{"cuisine/nikkei-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/nikkei-cuisine","n":"Peruvian-Japanese cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Peru","intro":[["Nikkei cuisine is a type of fusion cuisine that combines Japanese and Peruvian elements. Nikkei is the word used to describe immigrants of Japanese origin in Peru."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"nikkei-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikkei_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Nikkei Cuisine: Japanese Food the South American Way","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Luiz Hara, 2015","note":"Japanese-Peruvian and Japanese-Brazilian dishes from a London-based cook of Japanese-Brazilian descent.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781910254202","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781910254202"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/be/Pulpo_al_olivo_nikkei.jpg/500px-Pulpo_al_olivo_nikkei.jpg","w":500,"h":334,"by":"Lou Stejskal","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pulpo_al_olivo_nikkei.jpg"}},"cuisine/mughlai-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mughlai-cuisine","n":"Mughlai cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Mughlai cuisine consists of delicately-spiced dishes developed or popularised in the early-modern Indo-Persian cultural centres of the Mughal Empire. It represents a combination of cuisine of the Indian subcontinent with the cooking styles and recipes of Persian cuisine. The Mughals introduced foods such as stuffed meat and poultry, leavened bread, pilau, and dried fruits to the region. They brought cooking methods including the tandoor clay oven, the braising of meat, the practice of marinating meat in yoghurt, and the making of cheese. They incorporated India's spices and vegetables with these to create a distinctive cuisine. Sweetmeats too were much liked by the Mughals; these included halva, rice desserts, and falooda made with vermicelli in syrup."],["Mughal recipes are recorded in works from the Mughal era such as the Nuskha-i-Shahjahani, (\"Shah Jahan's Recipes\") and the Ḵẖulāṣat-i Mākūlāt u Mashrūbāt (\"Compendium of Things Eaten and Drunk\"). Sweetmeat recipes are recorded in the Ḵẖulāṣat-i Mākūlāt u Mashrūbāt and in a dedicated work, the Alwān-E-Niʿmat (\"Colours of the Table\")."],["In the modern era, Mughlai dishes have been adapted to diners' tastes. A well-known instance is the modification of dry yoghurt-marinated tandoor-baked chicken tikka with a creamy sauce to form chicken tikka masala, a British curry."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"mughlai-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mughlai_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/5/54/Murgh_Musallam_Steamed.JPG/500px-Murgh_Musallam_Steamed.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Miansari66","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murgh_Musallam_Steamed.JPG"}},"ingredient/bun":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/bun","n":"Bun","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A bun is a type of bread that is round and small enough that it can generally be eaten hand-held. Whether a bun is considered sweetened or unsweetened differs between countries: it is considered sweetened in England, Scotland and Wales, (except for the North-East of England where it is both) a savory bread in Northern Ireland, and either a sweet bread or a savory bread roll in the United States."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-bun"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bun"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Brazilian cuisine","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",[["Bauru (sandwich)","dish/bauru-sandwich"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Mozzarella","ingredient/mozzarella"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"],["Pão francês","ingredient/pao-frances"],["Roast beef","ingredient/roast-beef"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/1/10/Sesame_seed_hamburger_buns.jpg/500px-Sesame_seed_hamburger_buns.jpg","w":500,"h":325,"by":"jeffreyw","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sesame_seed_hamburger_buns.jpg"}},"dish/bibimbap":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bibimbap","n":"Bibimbap","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Bibimbap, sometimes romanised as bi bim bap or bi bim bop, is a Korean rice dish. It is served as a bowl of warm white rice topped with namul (sautéed or blanched seasoned vegetables) and gochujang (chili pepper paste). Egg and sliced meat (usually beef) are common additions, stirred together thoroughly just before eating. The term bibim means \"mixing\" and bap is cooked rice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bibimbap"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bibimbap"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Gochujang","ingredient/gochujang"],["Namul","ingredient/namul"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Maangchi","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"maangchi.com","note":"Maangchi's Korean recipe site and YouTube channel, one of the best known English sources for Korean cooking.","url":"https://www.maangchi.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Korean Bapsang","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Korean cuisine","links":[["Bap (rice dish)","dish/bap-rice-dish"],["Bibim-guksu","dish/bibim-guksu"],["Bosintang","dish/bosintang"],["Cheonggukjang","dish/cheonggukjang"],["Doenjang","dish/doenjang"],["Garak-guksu","dish/garak-guksu"],["Gochujang","dish/gochujang"],["Gopchang","dish/gopchang"],["Hwajeon","dish/hwajeon"],["Jijimi","dish/jijimi"],["Jokbal","dish/jokbal"],["Korean restaurant","dish/korean-restaurant"],["Maekjeok","dish/maekjeok"],["Maeun-tang","dish/maeun-tang"],["Makchang","dish/makchang"],["Mandu-guk","dish/mandu-guk"],["Mandu-gwa","dish/mandu-gwa"],["Meat jun","dish/meat-jun"],["Meju","dish/meju"],["Morkovcha","dish/morkovcha"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Seon (food)","dish/seon-food"],["Sungnyung","dish/sungnyung"],["Tteokbokki","dish/tteokbokki"],["Wakame","dish/wakame"],["Yakgwa","dish/yakgwa"],["Yukpo","dish/yukpo"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/f5/Korean.food-Bibimbap-02.jpg/500px-Korean.food-Bibimbap-02.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"abex (a flickr user)","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean.food-Bibimbap-02.jpg"}},"dish/dirty-rice":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/dirty-rice","n":"Dirty rice","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Dirty rice is a traditional Louisiana Creole dish made from white rice which gets a \"dirty\" color from being cooked with small pieces of pork, beef or chicken, green bell pepper, celery, and onion, and spiced with cayenne and black pepper.  Parsley and chopped green onions are common garnishes.  Dirty rice is most common in the Creole regions of southern Louisiana; however, it can also be found in other areas of the American South and referenced as \"chicken and rice,\" \"Cajun rice,\" or \"rice dressing\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-dirty-rice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirty_rice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Apium","ingredient/apium"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/5/54/Pork_chop_and_dirty_rice.jpg/500px-Pork_chop_and_dirty_rice.jpg","w":500,"h":345,"by":"Kent Wang","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pork_chop_and_dirty_rice.jpg"}},"dish/falooda":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/falooda","n":"Falooda","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 4 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["A falooda or faluda is a Mughlai cold dessert made with vermicelli. It has origins in the Persian dish faloodeh, variants of which are found across West, Central, South and Southeast Asia. Traditionally, it is prepared by mixing rose syrup, vermicelli, and sweet basil seeds with milk, often served with kulfi. The vermicelli used for preparing falooda is made from wheat, arrowroot, cornstarch, or sago."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-falooda"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falooda"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Afghan cuisine","cuisine/afghan-cuisine"],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"],["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine"],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Ocimum","ingredient/ocimum"],["Rose syrup","ingredient/rose-syrup"],["Rose water","ingredient/rose-water"],["Sweet basil","ingredient/sweet-basil"],["Vermicelli","ingredient/vermicelli"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Afghan Culture Unveiled","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"afghancultureunveiled.com","note":"Afghan-American Humaira Ghilzai's blog on Afghan food and culture.","url":"https://www.afghancultureunveiled.com/humaira-ghilzai/afghancooking/2013/01/our-aushak-recipe-in-print-food52.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Afghan Cooks","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"afghancooks.com","note":"Recipe blog dedicated to traditional Afghan dishes.","url":"https://afghancooks.com/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Swasthi's Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"indianhealthyrecipes.com","note":"Swasthi Shreekanth's long-running blog of everyday Indian home cooking from across the regions.","url":"https://www.indianhealthyrecipes.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hari Ghotra","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"harighotra.co.uk","note":"UK-based cook Hari Ghotra's recipe library and videos of home-style Indian dishes.","url":"https://www.harighotra.co.uk/indian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Afghan cuisine","links":[["Aushak","dish/aushak"],["Kabuli pulao","dish/kabuli-pulao"],["Nisholda","dish/nisholda"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Thali","dish/thali"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/69/%E0%A6%98%E0%A6%B0%E0%A7%87_%E0%A6%A4%E0%A7%88%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%BF_%E0%A6%AB%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B2%E0%A7%81%E0%A6%A6%E0%A6%BE.JPG/500px-%E0%A6%98%E0%A6%B0%E0%A7%87_%E0%A6%A4%E0%A7%88%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%BF_%E0%A6%AB%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B2%E0%A7%81%E0%A6%A6%E0%A6%BE.JPG","w":500,"h":1082,"by":"Kanchana Bose","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%A6%98%E0%A6%B0%E0%A7%87_%E0%A6%A4%E0%A7%88%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%BF_%E0%A6%AB%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B2%E0%A7%81%E0%A6%A6%E0%A6%BE.JPG"}},"dish/hobo-s":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/hobo-s","n":"Hobo's","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Hobo's Restaurant and Lounge, or simply Hobo's, was a restaurant, gay bar, and piano bar in Portland, Oregon's Old Town Chinatown, in the United States. Housed in a building with rare access to the Shanghai tunnels, the establishment served as a starting point for guided tours. The menu consisted of American cuisine including steakhouse fare. Hobo's was featured on the Food Network's Rachael Ray's Tasty Travels and the Travel Channel's Ghost Adventures, before closing during the COVID-19 pandemic."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-hobo-s"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hobo's"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/7/7f/Hobo%27s%2C_Portland%2C_2017.jpg/500px-Hobo%27s%2C_Portland%2C_2017.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Another Believer","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hobo's%2C_Portland%2C_2017.jpg"}},"ingredient/rib":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rib","n":"Rib","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["In vertebrate anatomy, ribs are the long curved bones which form the rib cage, part of the axial skeleton. In most tetrapods, ribs surround the thoracic cavity, enabling the lungs to expand and thus facilitate breathing by expanding the thoracic cavity. They serve to protect the lungs, heart, and other vital organs of the thorax. In some animals, especially snakes, ribs may provide support and protection for the entire body."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rib"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rib"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Konro","dish/konro"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Kaffir lime","ingredient/kaffir-lime"],["Keluak","ingredient/keluak"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Pangium edule","ingredient/pangium-edule"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Tamarind","ingredient/tamarind"]]}],"img":{"sr